        <h2 id="layout">Port layout</h2>
        <p>The following is the standard layout for a port directory:</p>

        <pre><code>example/
├── build.eme
├── depends
├── .checksums
├── .pkgfiles
├── files/
├── example.install
└── update.toml</code></pre>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>File</th>
                    <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>build.eme</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Required POSIX shell recipe.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>depends</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>One build/runtime dependency per line.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>.checksums</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Generated or reviewed source checksums.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>.pkgfiles</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Generated package file listing.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>files/</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Local files copied into the source workspace.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>example.install</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Optional unified package lifecycle hooks.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>update.toml</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Updater metadata such as provider and package identity.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="recipe">Recipe fields</h2>

        <pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env sh

# shellcheck disable=SC2034
description="Example package"
homepage="https://example.org"
maintainer="Vylen Linux Team &lt;you@example.org&gt;"

name=example
version=1.0.0
release=1
source="https://example.org/releases/${name}-${version}.tar.xz"

backup="etc/example.conf"
noextract="${name}-${version}.tar.xz"
</code></pre>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Field</th>
                    <th>Rules and effect</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>name</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Required, lowercase, and identical to the port directory name.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>version</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>
                        Required and must not contain <code>-</code>.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>release</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Required integer from 1 to 99.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>description</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Required non-empty package description.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>homepage</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Upstream or project homepage.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>maintainer</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Responsible port maintainer.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>source</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>
                        Space-separated URLs or
                        <code>filename::URL</code> mappings.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>backup</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Configuration paths preserved during upgrades.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>noextract</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Source filenames copied without archive extraction.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="source">Sources and checksums</h2>

        <p>
            <code>pkgbuild</code> downloads every URL with curl, retries
            failures, resumes readable <code>.partial</code> files, and stores
            completed files in <code>SOURCE_DIR</code>. A URL ending in a
            filename uses that basename; <code>filename::URL</code> selects a
            stable local name.
        </p>

        <pre><code>source="https://example.org/example-${version}.tar.xz
    https://example.org/config.example"

pkgbuild -g example  # generate checksums
pkgbuild -m example  # skip verification for deliberate local work</code></pre>

        <p>
            Normal builds verify checksums. Treat <code>-m</code> as a debugging
            escape hatch, not a release workflow.
        </p>

        <h2 id="build">The build function</h2>

        <pre><code>build() {
    # shellcheck disable=SC2164
    cd "${name}-${version}"

    ./configure \
        --prefix=/usr \
        --disable-static

    make
    make DESTDIR="${PKG}" install
}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Before calling <code>build</code>, pkgbuild creates
            <code>${SRC}</code> and <code>${PKG}</code>, copies
            <code>files/</code> into the source workspace, prepares compiler and
            language caches, exports toolchain parallelism, and runs an optional
            <code>prepare()</code> function if the recipe defines one. The
            recipe must install exclusively below <code>${PKG}</code>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="hooks">Package hooks</h2>

        <p>
            A port may contain one optional POSIX shell file named
            <code>PORT.install</code>, where <code>PORT</code> is the port name.
            For example, the <code>fish</code> port uses
            <code>fish/fish.install</code>. Emerald sources this single file and
            calls the function that matches the lifecycle stage and action.
        </p>

        <pre><code>#!/bin/sh

pre_install() {
    # $1: installed version; $2: target version
    :
}

post_install() {
    printf 'installed %s, target %s\n' "$1" "$2"
}

pre_upgrade() {
    :
}

post_upgrade() {
    :
}

pre_remove() {
    :
}

post_remove() {
    :
}</code></pre>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Function</th>
                    <th>When it runs</th>
                    <th>Typical use</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>pre_install()</code></td>
                    <td>Before a new package is installed.</td>
                    <td>Prepare state for the first installation.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>post_install()</code></td>
                    <td>After a new package is installed successfully.</td>
                    <td>Refresh databases or restart dependent services.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>pre_upgrade()</code></td>
                    <td>Before an installed package is upgraded.</td>
                    <td>Save state or prepare a transition.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>post_upgrade()</code></td>
                    <td>After an upgrade succeeds.</td>
                    <td>Migrate state or refresh generated data.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>pre_remove()</code></td>
                    <td>Before a package is removed.</td>
                    <td>Stop services or prepare data cleanup.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>post_remove()</code></td>
                    <td>After a package is removed successfully.</td>
                    <td>Clean package-specific state no longer owned.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            Every hook receives two positional arguments. Emerald removes the
            package release suffix before calling the function, so a package
            recorded as <code>2.4.0-1</code> is passed as <code>2.4.0</code>.
            <code>$1</code> is the installed version and <code>$2</code> is the
            target version being installed. The automatic values are:
        </p>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Hook</th>

                    <th>
                        <code>$1</code>
                    </th>

                    <th>
                        <code>$2</code>
                    </th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>pre_install()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>empty</td>
                    <td>new version</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>post_install()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>installed version</td>
                    <td>new version</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>pre_upgrade()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>old version</td>
                    <td>new version</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>post_upgrade()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>old version</td>
                    <td>new version</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>pre_remove()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>installed version</td>
                    <td>empty</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>post_remove()</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>installed version</td>
                    <td>empty</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            Emerald changes into the port directory before sourcing the install
            file. Hooks run as part of package operations, so they must be safe
            to run as root, use POSIX <code>sh</code> syntax, and remain short
            and idempotent. A missing install file or missing function is
            ignored.
        </p>

        <p>To run a hook explicitly for a port, use:</p>

        <pre><code>emerald hook pre install example
emerald hook post install example
emerald hook pre upgrade example
emerald hook post upgrade example
emerald hook pre remove example
emerald hook post remove example</code></pre>

        <p>
            Do not put ordinary files into hooks. Configuration files belong in
            <code>files/</code>, package dependencies belong in
            <code>depends</code>, and files that must survive upgrades belong in
            the recipe's <code>backup</code> value. A hook is for a transition
            action, not a replacement for the package payload.
        </p>

        <h2 id="pipeline">Full pipeline</h2>

        <ol>
            <li>Load configuration and resolve custom paths and job count.</li>
            <li>Validate writable source, package, and work directories.</li>

            <li>
                Source and validate <code>build.eme</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Construct the package filename
                <code>name-version-release.epkg.tar.compression</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Handle early-exit modes: package file list, download, extract,
                checksum generation, or clean.
            </li>

            <li>
                Acquire a per-package lock at
                <code>/tmp/pkgbuild.name.lock</code>.
            </li>

            <li>Fetch sources, verify checksums, unpack archives, and copy local files.</li>

            <li>
                Run optional <code>prepare()</code>, then <code>build()</code>,
                with tracing unless quiet.
            </li>

            <li>
                Automatically add <code>/etc/conf.d/*</code> files to backup
                metadata when not already listed.
            </li>

            <li>
                Lint and prune the staging tree according to strip,
                documentation, locale, libtool, and cache policy.
            </li>

            <li>
                Compress the staged tree, write the package archive, and
                generate <code>.pkgfiles</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
                Remove the workspace unless <code>--keep-work</code> was
                requested.
            </li>

            <li>
                Install, upgrade, or reinstall through <code>pkgadd</code> when
                requested, then release the lock.
            </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="metadata">Review checklist</h2>

        <p>
            Confirm that the name is lowercase and matches the directory,
            version contains no hyphen, release is 1–99, description is present,
            sources are stable, checksums are current, dependencies are
            complete, and no file escapes <code>${PKG}</code>. Build once with
            <code>--keep-work</code>, inspect the staged result and generated
            file list, then test installation and removal.
        </p>

        <h2 id="advanced">Advanced build state and pruning</h2>

        <p>
            Recipes can persist tool-generated configuration between builds
            through the cbuild helpers.
            <code>cbuild_save source [name]</code> copies a generated
            configuration into <code>CBUILD_DIR/name.cfg</code>;
            <code>cbuild_restore destination [name]</code> restores it; and
            <code>cbuild_options [name]</code> sources an optional
            <code>CBUILD_DIR/name.opt</code> file. These helpers are useful for
            configure-cache-like workflows, but the saved state must not make a
            build depend on an undocumented machine.
        </p>

        <pre><code>cbuild_save config.cache example
cbuild_restore config.cache example
cbuild_options example</code></pre>

        <p>
            Before packaging, pkgbuild can strip binaries, compress info and
            manual pages, remove unwanted documentation and locale data, remove
            libtool artifacts, and preserve files listed in <code>backup</code>.
            OpenRC configuration files under <code>${PKG}/etc/conf.d/</code> are
            automatically added to backup metadata unless already listed.
        </p>

        <p>
            Every active build creates <code>/tmp/pkgbuild.name.lock</code>.
            This prevents two builds of the same package from racing over one
            workspace or package output. Interrupt handling removes the lock and
            performs normal workspace cleanup.
        </p>
